    'Set <strong>weekly targets</strong>, not just end-of-term ones, and leave slack for the weeks that go wrong.',
    'A short weekly review keeps the plan alive; a plan you never revisit is just decoration.',
  ]}
  toc={[
    { id: 'why', label: 'Why plan a whole semester' },
    { id: 'build', label: 'Build it in one session' },
    { id: 'weekly', label: 'From semester to week' },
    { id: 'adjust', label: 'Review and adjust' },
    { id: 'faq', label: 'FAQ' },
  ]}
  tools={['/tools/timetable-maker/', '/tools/assignment-planner/', '/tools/exam-countdown/']}
  faqs={[
    { q: 'How do I make a study plan for the whole semester?', a: 'In week one, gather every syllabus, put all deadlines and exam dates on one calendar, mark the busy collision weeks, and set weekly targets that start big projects early. Then review it for ten minutes each week to keep it current.' },
    { q: 'When should I make my semester plan?', a: 'In the first week, as soon as you have every syllabus. Planning early is what lets you spot the weeks where several deadlines collide and start the work before they arrive.' },
    { q: 'How detailed should a semester plan be?', a: 'Detailed on dates and milestones, loose on daily specifics. Fix the deadlines and weekly targets now; decide exact daily tasks during each weekly review, when you know how the previous week went.' },
